text:
Getting data

data &lt;- read_rds(SRC_FILE)

Let’s check the range of wavenumbers:

data |&gt; 
  select(starts_with("wn")) |&gt; 
  names() |&gt; 
  parse_number() |&gt; 
  range()

[1]  600 4000

Pre-processing recipe
The recipe is the one from Feature engineering, without step_resample(). OSSL spectra are already on one common grid, every 2 cm⁻¹ from 600 to 4000 cm⁻¹, as the range check above shows.

spc_cols &lt;- names(data)[startsWith(names(data), "wn_")]
meta_cols &lt;- setdiff(names(data), c(TARGET, spc_cols))

rec &lt;- recipe(data) |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(TARGET),  new_role = "outcome") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(spc_cols), new_role = "predictor") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(meta_cols), new_role = "metadata") |&gt; 
  step_log(all_outcomes(), base = 10) |&gt;
  step_snv() |&gt; 
  step_sg(w = SG_W, p = SG_P, m = SG_M)

Why the page 6 workflow cannot predict new spectra
A recipe runs at two moments. It runs when the workflow is fitted, on the training data. It runs again every time the workflow predicts, on the new data. The same steps apply at both moments, which is the point: a new spectrum gets exactly the resampling, SNV and Savitzky-Golay filter the training spectra got.
Steps on predictors work at both moments. Two parts of the page 6 recipe do not. The chunks below rebuild that workflow, fit it on the training set, and try to predict new_spectra.

spc_cols  &lt;- names(data)[startsWith(names(data), "wn_")]
meta_cols &lt;- setdiff(names(data), c(TARGET, spc_cols))

pls_spec &lt;- pls(num_comp = NUM_COMP) |&gt;
  set_engine("mixOmics") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")

rec_page6 &lt;- recipe(data) |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(TARGET),    new_role = "outcome") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(spc_cols),  new_role = "predictor") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(meta_cols), new_role = "metadata") |&gt;
  step_log(all_outcomes(), base = 10) |&gt;
  step_resample(grid = RESAMPLING_GRID) |&gt;
  step_snv() |&gt;
  step_sg(w = SG_W, p = SG_P, m = SG_M)

wf_page6 &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec_page6) |&gt;
  add_model(pls_spec) |&gt;
  fit(training(split))


predict(wf_page6, new_spectra)

Error in `hardhat::forge()`:
! The required columns "source", "clay_perc", "pH_H20",
  "carbon_org_perc", "potassium_cmolkg", and "phosporus_mgkg" are missing.


The first problem is the metadata columns. By default, recipes requires every column it saw at fitting time, whatever its role. The lab columns have the role “metadata”, so the recipe asks for them. This default is a safety net: some steps use non-predictor columns, and a missing column should stop the prediction rather than pass unnoticed. Here, the lab columns are only carried along. update_role_requirements("metadata", bake = FALSE) says so: the recipe needs them at fitting time, but not when it bakes new data.

wf_meta &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec_page6 |&gt; update_role_requirements("metadata", bake = FALSE)) |&gt;
  add_model(pls_spec) |&gt;
  fit(training(split))

predict(wf_meta, new_spectra)

Error in `step_log()`:
! The following required column is missing from `new_data`:
  carbon_tot_perc.


The second problem is step_log(). It acts on the outcome, and it also runs at prediction time. A new sample has no carbon value to log. This is a general limit of tidymodels: a recipe is for transforms that apply to every sample, and the outcome exists only for the training samples.
The fix is to log the outcome before the recipe, in the data itself. The model is the same. It still learns and predicts log10 values. The recipe now contains only steps that a new spectrum can go through.

Model
The recipe is the one Feature engineering builds step by step, here with carbon_tot_perc as the outcome.

spc_cols &lt;- names(data)[startsWith(names(data), "wn_")]
meta_cols &lt;- setdiff(names(data), c(TARGET, spc_cols))

rec &lt;- recipe(data) |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(TARGET),  new_role = "outcome") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(spc_cols), new_role = "predictor") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(meta_cols), new_role = "metadata") |&gt; 
  step_log(all_outcomes(), base = 10) |&gt;
  step_resample(grid = RESAMPLING_GRID) |&gt; 
  step_snv() |&gt; 
  step_sg(w = SG_W, p = SG_P, m = SG_M)

pls() specifies a partial least squares regression, fitted with the mixOmics engine. num_comp = tune() leaves the number of components open. tune_grid() fills it in later.

pls_spec &lt;- pls(num_comp = tune()) |&gt;
  set_engine("mixOmics") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")

A workflow bundles the recipe and the model. Both are then fitted together inside each fold.

wf &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec) |&gt;
  add_model(pls_spec)

Building the working sample
One pipe turns the full library into the working sample and saves it. Each |&gt; passes the result of one line to the next.

set.seed(123)
data |&gt; 
    filter(!is.na(k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg)) |&gt; 
    filter(if_all(starts_with("scan_mir"), ~ !is.na(.x))) |&gt; 
    filter(k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg &gt;= kex_threshold) |&gt;
    slice_sample(prop=0.1) |&gt; 
    select(id.layer_uuid_txt, k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg, starts_with("scan_mir")) |&gt;
    rename_with(~ str_replace(.x, "scan_mir\\.(\\d+)_abs", "wn_\\1"), .cols = starts_with("scan_mir")) |&gt; 
    rename(smp_id = id.layer_uuid_txt, kex = k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg) |&gt;
    write_rds(dest, compress = "xz")


Line by line

set.seed(123) fixes the random number generator. slice_sample() then draws the same samples every time the page runs.
filter(!is.na(k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg)) keeps only rows where Kex was measured.
filter(if_all(starts_with("scan_mir"), ~ !is.na(.x))) keeps only rows with a complete MIR spectrum. starts_with("scan_mir") selects every MIR column. ~ !is.na(.x) is a short way to write a function: .x stands for each column in turn, and the function returns TRUE where a value is present. if_all() keeps a row only if the function returns TRUE for every selected column. This drops rows that have only a VisNIR spectrum, or none.
filter(k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg &gt;= kex_threshold) drops values below the detection threshold, twice the MDL.
slice_sample(prop = 0.1) keeps a random 10% of the remaining rows. That gives 4,548 samples, which is enough to work with and small enough to fit a model quickly in class.
select(id.layer_uuid_txt, k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg, starts_with("scan_mir")) keeps only the columns the model needs: the sample identifier, the target and the MIR spectrum. It drops everything else, such as site data and the other soil properties.
rename_with(~ str_replace(.x, "scan_mir\\.(\\d+)_abs", "wn_\\1"), .cols = starts_with("scan_mir")) renames every MIR column, for example scan_mir.600_abs to wn_600. rename_with() applies a function to the names of the columns in .cols. str_replace() uses a regular expression. \\. matches a literal dot. (\\d+) captures the digits of the wavenumber. \\1 puts those digits back in the new name. The backslashes are doubled because R strings need \\ to write one \. The new names match those of the RT data. The recipe steps and plotting functions from earlier pages then work unchanged.
rename(smp_id = id.layer_uuid_txt, kex = k.ext_usda.a725_cmolc.kg) gives the two remaining columns short names. The new name goes on the left of =, the old name on the right.
write_rds(dest, compress = "xz") saves the result as an R object. xz compression is slow, but it gives the smallest file: about 20 MB here.

Models
The recipe is the one from the other pages, with tune() in place of the three SG values. A second recipe leaves out step_sg(). It answers a simpler question: does SG help at all?

spc_cols &lt;- names(data)[startsWith(names(data), "wn_")]
meta_cols &lt;- setdiff(names(data), c(TARGET, spc_cols))

rec_base &lt;- recipe(data) |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(TARGET),  new_role = "outcome") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(spc_cols), new_role = "predictor") |&gt;
  update_role(all_of(meta_cols), new_role = "metadata") |&gt;
  step_log(all_outcomes(), base = 10) |&gt;
  step_resample(grid = RESAMPLING_GRID) |&gt;
  step_snv()

rec_sg &lt;- rec_base |&gt;
  step_sg(w = tune(), p = tune(), m = tune())

pls_spec &lt;- pls(num_comp = tune()) |&gt;
  set_engine("mixOmics") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")

wf_base &lt;- workflow() |&gt; add_recipe(rec_base) |&gt; add_model(pls_spec)
wf_sg   &lt;- workflow() |&gt; add_recipe(rec_sg)   |&gt; add_model(pls_spec)

Each SG setting is crossed with 2 to 20 PLS components, as on Evaluating performance.

grid_sg &lt;- expand_grid(sg_settings, num_comp = NUM_COMP)

Each SG setting has a cost. It changes the spectra, so in every fold the recipe must be prepped again and PLS fitted again. The number of components adds almost no cost. PLS builds its components one after the other, and adding a component does not change the ones before it. So a model fitted with 20 components already contains the models with 2 to 19 components: to predict with 5 components, it uses only the first 5. tune_grid() uses this, and fits PLS only once per SG setting in each fold. With the model without SG, that is 8 settings × 100 folds, 800 fits.
cache: true stores the result of the two tuning chunks on disk. Later renders reuse it, as long as the chunk code and the objects hashed in cache.extra are unchanged. rlang::hash() turns those objects into one short string, which changes whenever any of them changes. To run the tuning again, delete the tuning-savitzky-golay_cache folder.

res_base &lt;- tune_grid(
  wf_base,
  resamples = folds,
  grid = tibble(num_comp = NUM_COMP),
  metrics = metric_set(rmse)
)


res_sg &lt;- tune_grid(
  wf_sg,
  resamples = folds,
  grid = grid_sg,
  metrics = metric_set(rmse)
)
